import{_ as o,o as t,c as d,a1 as r}from"./chunks/framework.DceXOx8u.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Security","description":"","frontmatter":{"head":[["link",{"rel":"canonical","href":"https://andresayac.github.io/botmesh/guide/security"}],["meta",{"property":"og:url","content":"https://andresayac.github.io/botmesh/guide/security"}]]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guide/security.md","filePath":"guide/security.md"}'),s={name:"guide/security.md"};function a(c,e,i,n,h,l){return t(),d("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<h1 id="security" tabindex="-1">Security <a class="header-anchor" href="#security" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Security&quot;">​</a></h1><p>What you have to configure, what the framework verifies for you on each untrusted surface, and the risks that remain. To report a vulnerability, follow <a href="https://github.com/andresayac/botmesh/blob/main/SECURITY.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>SECURITY.md</code></a>. For who the framework protects against what, see the <a href="./threat-model">Threat model</a>.</p><h2 id="production-checklist" tabindex="-1">Production checklist <a class="header-anchor" href="#production-checklist" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Production checklist&quot;">​</a></h2><p>What is left to you. The rest of this page explains each point.</p><ul><li><strong>Secrets out of the code.</strong> Provider tokens, API keys, app secrets and webhook secrets come from the environment or a secret manager. The <code>sessions/</code> folder (WhatsApp keys), <code>.env</code>, the store files and <code>*.qr.png</code> are never committed or baked into an image.</li><li><strong>Webhooks verify their sender.</strong> Configure the channel&#39;s secret (<code>appSecret</code> and <code>verifyToken</code> for the Cloud API, <code>webhookSecret</code> for Telegram, the auth token for Twilio…). Every provider refuses to start without it unless you set <code>allowUnsignedWebhooks: true</code>: never set that in production. See <a href="#webhook-authentication">Webhook authentication</a>.</li><li><strong>HTTPS everywhere.</strong> Most platforms sign no timestamp, so a captured request can be replayed; TLS stops the capture.</li><li><strong>Raw body for signatures.</strong> No JSON body parser in front of webhook routes: signatures are checked over the received bytes (<a href="./server">Webhook server</a>).</li><li><strong>No user text as a media source.</strong> A string or <code>{ path }</code> media source is read from local disk, and so is a non-<code>http</code> <code>{ url }</code> on Baileys. Pass <code>https</code> URLs you checked yourself or Buffers built from data you trust: only Baileys fetches remote media through <code>@botmesh/http</code>; the other transports hand the link to their server or platform (see <a href="#residual-risks">Residual risks</a>).</li><li><strong>Personal data stays out of logs and traces.</strong> Keep <code>logIds: &#39;hash&#39;</code> (the default) and <code>telemetry.ids: &#39;hash&#39;</code> with a secret <code>idKey</code>; leave <code>telemetry.errorMessages</code> off.</li><li><strong>Protect the store and the journals</strong> like the messages they hold: restricted file permissions, encrypted backups, authenticated Redis with TLS on untrusted networks.</li><li><strong>Operator endpoints are private.</strong> Health, metrics and any admin API listen on a private interface or behind authentication.</li><li><strong>Answer erasure requests</strong> with <code>bot.deleteSubject(userId)</code> (and <code>bot.exportSubject(userId)</code> for access requests).</li></ul><h2 id="security-audit-2026-10" tabindex="-1">Security audit (2026-10) <a class="header-anchor" href="#security-audit-2026-10" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Security audit (2026-10)&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The rest of this page is the result of the October 2026 audit of the newer surfaces (webhook ingress, the manager REST API, the newer channels and plugins). Most audited packages are public from the beta; <code>manager</code> and <code>tiktok</code> are still private. The published ones are listed in <a href="./production#which-packages-are-published">Going to production</a>.</p><p><strong>Scope:</strong> <code>@botmesh/server</code>, <code>twilio</code>, <code>gohighlevel</code>, <code>gupshup</code>, <code>evolution</code>, <code>wppconnect</code>, <code>email</code>, <code>tiktok</code>, <code>chatwoot</code>, <code>voice</code>, <code>rag</code>, <code>manager</code> (<code>/v1</code> REST and the older <code>createManagerApi</code>), <code>dialogflow</code>, <code>moderation</code>, the webhook paths of <code>meta</code>/<code>messenger</code>/<code>instagram</code> (<code>@botmesh/graph-core</code>) and <code>telegram</code>, plus the shared HTTP client <code>@botmesh/http</code>.</p><p>Every issue fixed during the audit has a regression test (look for <code>security-audit</code> in the package tests).</p><h3 id="webhook-authentication" tabindex="-1">Webhook authentication <a class="header-anchor" href="#webhook-authentication" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Webhook authentication&quot;">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Channel</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Raw bytes</th><th>Constant time</th><th>Replay window</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Meta (Cloud API, Messenger, Instagram)</td><td><code>X-Hub-Signature-256</code> HMAC-SHA256 with <code>appSecret</code>; <code>GET</code> handshake with <code>verifyToken</code></td><td>yes (a parsed body is rejected)</td><td>yes (handshake token too)</td><td>Meta sends no signed timestamp — dedupe by message id</td></tr><tr><td>Telegram</td><td><code>X-Telegram-Bot-Api-Secret-Token</code> (<code>webhookSecret</code>)</td><td>n/a (shared secret)</td><td>yes</td><td>none from the platform</td></tr><tr><td>Twilio</td><td><code>X-Twilio-Signature</code> HMAC-SHA1 over the exact URL + sorted params; JSON bodies through <code>bodySHA256</code></td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>none from the platform</td></tr><tr><td>TikTok</td><td><code>TikTok-Signature: t=…,s=…</code> HMAC-SHA256 over <code>t.rawBody</code></td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td><strong>yes</strong>, ±300 s (<code>signatureToleranceSec</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>HighLevel</td><td><code>X-GHL-Signature</code> Ed25519 (legacy RSA <code>X-WH-Signature</code> only when you configure its key)</td><td>yes</td><td>n/a (signature verify)</td><td>none enforced; see residual risks</td></tr><tr><td>Gupshup, WPPConnect</td><td>shared token in the callback URL or a header (they do not sign)</td><td>n/a</td><td>yes</td><td>none</td></tr><tr><td>Evolution</td><td>shared header secret or HS256 JWT (<code>exp</code> required, ±30 s skew)</td><td>n/a</td><td>yes</td><td>JWT <code>exp</code></td></tr><tr><td>Chatwoot</td><td>shared token and/or HMAC-SHA256 (<code>x-chatwoot-signature</code> over <code>x-chatwoot-timestamp</code> + body, ±300 s)</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td><strong>yes</strong>, ±300 s; the untimed form needs <code>webhookAllowUntimedSignature</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Every provider refuses to start without credentials unless you set <code>allowUnsignedWebhooks: true</code> (or <code>insecureNoWebhookAuth</code> in Chatwoot), and then logs a warning. Inbound ids are deduplicated by the core (<code>chatId</code> + message id), which also absorbs replays of an already-seen message while it is in the dedupe window.</p><h3 id="verified" tabindex="-1">Verified <a class="header-anchor" href="#verified" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Verified&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Raw-body signatures.</strong> Signatures are computed over the received bytes, never over re-serialized JSON. <code>@botmesh/server</code> reads the raw body itself and answers <code>500</code> if a body parser consumed it first.</li><li><strong>Ingress limits</strong> (<code>@botmesh/server</code>): body size (<code>413</code>, checked against <code>Content-Length</code> and while streaming), body and ingest timeouts (<code>408</code>/<code>503</code>), a concurrency cap (<code>503</code> + <code>Retry-After</code>), header-injection-safe response headers (CR/LF/NUL rejected) and logs with metadata only (no bodies, no error messages that could quote a body).</li><li><strong>Outbound SSRF.</strong> Every fetch to a URL that comes from a webhook or a user (media downloads, attachments, STT/TTS, embeddings, Dialogflow) goes through <code>secureFetch</code>: HTTPS only by default, no credentials in the URL, DNS answers checked against private ranges and the socket pinned to the checked IP (no DNS rebinding), every redirect re-validated, timeouts and size limits. Rejections are a <code>SecureHttpError</code> whose <code>code</code> says why: <code>INVALID_URL</code>, <code>INVALID_OPTIONS</code>, <code>UNSAFE_URL</code>, <code>UNSAFE_HOST</code>, <code>TIMEOUT</code>, <code>RESPONSE_TOO_LARGE</code>, <code>TOO_MANY_REDIRECTS</code>, or <code>HTTP_STATUS</code> (a non-2xx from <code>fetchBuffer</code>, with the code in <code>error.status</code>).</li><li><strong>Manager REST <code>/v1</code>.</strong> Mandatory tokens (≥ 16 chars, compared as SHA-256 digests without early exit), per-route scopes, tenant isolation (other tenants&#39; bots answer <code>404</code>), token-bucket rate limiting, <code>1 MiB</code> JSON body limit, <code>415</code> for non-JSON, allowlisted fields, length-capped strings, secrets redacted from echoed config and generic <code>500</code> messages. Bot and tenant ids are restricted to <code>[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9_.-]{0,63}</code> (no <code>..</code>) before they become file names.</li><li><strong>JSON flow expressions.</strong> A recursive-descent parser: no <code>eval</code>/<code>Function</code>, no regexes, whitelisted functions only, <code>__proto__</code>/<code>prototype</code>/<code>constructor</code> rejected, own-property paths only, at most 1000 chars / 256 tokens / depth 32. Templates (<code>{{ path }}</code>) only resolve paths.</li><li><strong>Email.</strong> Native <code>headers</code> must be single-line and cannot override the provider&#39;s own (<code>From</code>, <code>To</code>, <code>Message-ID</code>, loop protection...); native attachments must be in memory (no <code>path</code>/<code>href</code> that nodemailer would read or fetch itself).</li><li><strong>RAG loaders</strong> read only operator-given paths, skip symlinks and dotfiles while walking directories and cap each file (20 MiB).</li><li><strong>Logging.</strong> Grepped every <code>logger.*</code> call in the audited packages: no tokens, QR payloads, message bodies or addresses; errors are logged by class or by provider-reported reason. The core&#39;s own lines carry chat and sender ids through <code>BotConfig.logIds</code>: hashed by default (HMAC, 16 characters, the same hash as the trace spans; spans with <code>telemetry.ids: &#39;raw&#39;</code> do not make the logs raw), <code>&#39;raw&#39;</code> for local debugging, <code>&#39;omit&#39;</code> to drop them.</li></ul><h3 id="fixed-during-the-audit" tabindex="-1">Fixed during the audit <a class="header-anchor" href="#fixed-during-the-audit" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Fixed during the audit&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong><code>@botmesh/http</code>: IPv6 SSRF bypass.</strong> <code>isPrivateAddress</code> did not understand IPv6 that embeds an IPv4: <code>https://[::ffff:7f00:1]/</code> (the form <code>URL</code> normalizes <code>::ffff:127.0.0.1</code> to), <code>::127.0.0.1</code>, NAT64 (<code>64:ff9b::/96</code>), 6to4 (<code>2002::/16</code>) and Teredo reached internal addresses. It now expands IPv6 and judges the embedded IPv4; unparseable literals fail closed.</li><li><strong><code>@botmesh/http</code>: redirects.</strong> Cross-origin hops now also drop credential-looking headers (<code>apikey</code>, <code>x-api-key</code>, <code>xi-api-key</code>, <code>x-auth-token</code>...), not only <code>Authorization</code>/<code>Cookie</code>; a <code>303</code> (and a <code>301</code>/<code>302</code> after <code>POST</code>) continues as a body-less <code>GET</code>, like <code>fetch</code>.</li><li><strong><code>@botmesh/manager</code>: SSRF through <code>send</code>.</strong> <code>POST /v1/bots/{id}/send</code> accepted any <code>https</code> media URL, and transports such as Baileys download <code>{ url }</code> media themselves (outside <code>secureFetch</code>). Hosts that are or resolve to internal addresses are now rejected with <code>400</code> (<code>allowPrivateMediaHosts: true</code> to opt out, <code>mediaHostResolver</code> for tests).</li><li><strong><code>@botmesh/baileys</code>: SSRF through redirects.</strong> Baileys downloaded <code>{ url }</code> media with plain <code>fetch</code>, which follows redirects without checking them, so a public URL answering <code>302</code> to <code>http://169.254.169.254/…</code> got past the REST host check. The provider now downloads remote media itself through <code>@botmesh/http</code> (every hop vetted, socket pinned to the vetted address, 64 MiB and 60 s caps) and hands Baileys a Buffer. Tune it with <code>remoteMedia</code> (<code>maxBytes</code>, <code>timeoutMs</code>, <code>allowHttp</code>); <code>remoteMedia.allowPrivateHosts: true</code> restores the old pass-through for media served from your own network.</li><li><strong><code>@botmesh/manager</code>: error leak.</strong> The older <code>createManagerApi</code> returned the error message on <code>500</code>; it now answers <code>&quot;start failed&quot;</code>/<code>&quot;stop failed&quot;</code> and hands the error to <code>onError</code>.</li><li><strong><code>@botmesh/graph-core</code>: verify token.</strong> The <code>GET</code> handshake compared the token with <code>===</code> and accepted an empty configured token; now constant-time, non-empty and string-only.</li><li><strong><code>@botmesh/email</code>: header injection.</strong> Subjects and display names decoded from RFC 2047 could carry CR/LF into the reply&#39;s <code>Subject</code>/<code>To</code>; they are collapsed to one line. Native <code>cc</code>/<code>bcc</code> must be plain addresses.</li><li><strong><code>@botmesh/gohighlevel</code>: cross-location replay.</strong> HighLevel signs every app&#39;s webhooks with one platform key, so a body signed for another location verified here too; events without (or with another) <code>locationId</code> are ignored.</li><li><strong><code>@botmesh/chatwoot</code>: agent attachments.</strong> Only absolute <code>http(s)</code> URLs are forwarded (Baileys reads any other <code>{ url }</code> from the local disk), file names lose path separators and a malformed <code>%</code> no longer drops the whole message.</li><li><strong><code>@botmesh/moderation</code>: quadratic link scan.</strong> <code>findLinks</code> compared every bare invite with every link (≈2.5 s on a 200 KB message); it is linear now.</li></ul><h3 id="residual-risks" tabindex="-1">Residual risks <a class="header-anchor" href="#residual-risks" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Residual risks&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>No platform replay window</strong> for Meta, Telegram, Twilio, HighLevel, Gupshup and WPPConnect: they sign no timestamp (or HighLevel&#39;s is not enforced, since its retries may reuse it). A captured, valid request can be replayed; the core&#39;s id dedupe only covers its in-memory window. Keep webhook endpoints on HTTPS so requests cannot be captured.</li><li><strong>Tokens in URLs</strong> (Gupshup, WPPConnect, Chatwoot <code>?token=</code>) end up in reverse-proxy and access logs. Prefer the header form where the platform allows it, and scrub query strings from your logs.</li><li><strong>Twilio URL reconstruction.</strong> Without <code>webhookUrl</code>, the signed URL is rebuilt from <code>X-Forwarded-Host</code>/<code>Host</code>. Set <code>webhookUrl</code> (or use <code>ingestRequest</code> with the exact URL) behind proxies you do not control.</li><li><strong>Operator-written regexes.</strong> <code>moderation</code> word filters and invite patterns accept <code>RegExp</code>s and run them on every message: a catastrophic-backtracking pattern is a ReDoS. Use plain strings, or linear patterns only.</li><li><strong>String media sources are local paths.</strong> In <code>ctx.send</code>/<code>bot.send</code>, a string or <code>{ path }</code> media source is read from disk, and Baileys reads a non-<code>http</code> <code>{ url }</code> from disk too. Never pass user-controlled text as a media source; the REST API and Chatwoot already restrict it to <code>http(s)</code> URLs.</li><li><strong>Transports that fetch by themselves.</strong> Evolution and WPPConnect pass <code>{ url }</code> media to their own server, which downloads it (and follows redirects) from its network; Cloud API, Messenger, Instagram, Twilio, Gupshup and HighLevel hand the link to the platform. The REST API vets the first host, but a redirect is resolved by that other server, outside <code>secureFetch</code>. Host those servers where an internal redirect target is unreachable, or send Buffers. Baileys is covered (it downloads through <code>@botmesh/http</code>) unless you set <code>remoteMedia.allowPrivateHosts: true</code>.</li><li><strong>In-memory state grows with contacts</strong>: LID/PN identity maps (Evolution, WPPConnect, Baileys), moderation warning counters and the email suppression list are not capped. Other per-chat maps are bounded.</li><li><strong>Upstream error text.</strong> STT/TTS/Dialogflow errors include the provider&#39;s short error message (≤ 200 chars); providers mask keys there, but it is third-party text.</li><li><strong>Legacy <code>createManagerApi</code></strong> is unauthenticated unless you pass <code>auth</code> (it warns once). Prefer <code>createRestApi</code>.</li><li><strong>Windows device names.</strong> Bot ids like <code>con</code> or <code>nul</code> pass the id pattern; avoid them if you persist bots on Windows.</li></ul>',18)])])}const p=o(s,[["render",a]]);export{g as __pageData,p as default};
